/T (During takeoff, the lift rotors provide vertical thrust. The pusher then accelerates the aircraft while rotor thrust is reduced as the wing starts carrying the load. The reference design assumes a stall speed of 18.8 m/s and a cruise speed of 24 m/s \(86 km/h\). Full rotor shutdown is therefore proposed only after adequate speed margin is achieved. Landing reverses the sequence. These are analytically modeled operating phases, not demonstrated Mars flight performance.)
/E (During takeoff, the lift rotors provide vertical thrust. The pusher then accelerates the aircraft while rotor thrust is reduced as the wing starts carrying the load. The reference design assumes a stall speed of 18.8 m/s and a cruise speed of 24 m/s \(86 km/h\). Full rotor shutdown is therefore proposed only after adequate speed margin is achieved. Landing reverses the sequence. These are analytically modeled operating phases, not demonstrated Mars flight performance.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [9]
